<%# The two provider doors. Local: native (default false).

    House buttons, official marks (design review D4, story 0015): the field,
    border, radius and type come from the token table; the providers are
    recognisable by their logos alone. Google's G is the one non-token colour in
    the product and DESIGN.md records the exception.

    TWO SHAPES, ONE APPEARANCE, and the difference is not cosmetic.

      web     a FORM posting to /auth/:provider. OmniAuth's request phase is
              POST-only (`omniauth.rb`), which is what omniauth-rails_csrf_protection
              guards, so a link would not start the flow at all. `turbo: false`
              because the response is a redirect into middleware, not a
              Turbo-navigable document.

      native  a LINK to /auth/start/:provider. The shell's route decision
              handler matches that path and opens it in an
              ASWebAuthenticationSession instead of navigating — a form submit
              is not a `VisitProposal` and would sail straight past it into the
              web view, which is exactly where Google answers 403.

    The native branch renders ONLY for a shell whose user agent carries a
    version, because the version and the interception ship in the same binary.
    `CornersController#show` owns that test; this partial owns the markup. %>
<div class="signin__doors">
  <% providers = [
       [ "google_oauth2", "Continue with Google" ],
       [ "apple",         "Continue with Apple" ]
     ] %>

  <% providers.each do |provider, label| %>
    <% if local_assigns[:native] %>
      <%= link_to auth_start_path(provider), class: "signin__door", data: { turbo: false } do %>
        <%= render "sessions/provider_mark", provider: provider %><%= label %>
      <% end %>
    <% else %>
      <%= button_to "/auth/#{provider}", method: :post, class: "signin__door",
            data: { turbo: false } do %>
        <%= render "sessions/provider_mark", provider: provider %><%= label %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
</div>
